\title{The Identifiability Theory of Layer Ablation\\[0.4em]
\large Solo profiles, downstream contraction, and an arity hierarchy for
ablation experiments in layered stochastic maps}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Layer-wise ablation is the standard instrument for attributing importance to the
layers of a deep stack: break one layer, measure the degradation at the output,
repeat. We develop an exact theory of what this instrument can and cannot
measure, in a finite probabilistic model in which a layer is a Markov channel on
a finite state space and damage is total variation distance. Three functionals
are distinguished: the \emph{point cost} of a layer (the damage it does to its
own output law at the intact upstream state), the \emph{solo cost} (the damage
visible at the output of the stack when that layer alone is ablated --- what the
experiment reports), and the \emph{joint cost} (the damage when the whole stack
is ablated --- what deployment cares about).

We prove: (i) a context-shift inequality
$\dtv(f_*\mu,p_*\mu)\le\dtv(f_*\nu,p_*\nu)+2\dtv(\mu,\nu)$ isolating upstream
drift as the sole source of epistasis, together with a telescoping hybrid bound
and a depth-additive budget valid exactly when per-layer perturbations are small
\emph{uniformly over upstream states}; (ii) non-identifiability --- two prunings
of a single depth-$24$ stack with identical, identically zero solo profiles
whose joint costs are $0.017$ and $1$, plus a stack whose two solo costs are
both maximal and whose joint cost is $0$; (iii) a masking theorem
$\so_j\le\delta^{m_j}\pt_j$ derived from the finite Dobrushin contraction
theorem and attained exactly in an affine two-state family, so flat solo
profiles are \emph{predicted} by contraction; (iv) an exact converse ---
identifiability at a lossless suffix, in particular unconditionally at the final
layer; (v) a geometric sub-additivity bound $\jt\le c\sum_{i<n}\delta^i\le
c/(1-\delta)$, independent of depth and attained, turning the observed
sub-additivity ratio into an estimator of the contraction coefficient; and (vi)
an arity hierarchy --- for every $m$, a depth-$24$ stack on which every ablation
of at most $m$ layers returns exactly $0$ while a single experiment of arity
$m+1$ returns the true damage, so no fixed-arity ablation protocol is sound. A
fractional refinement computes the minimal informative arity in closed form from
the contraction spectrum.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} total variation; Markov channel; Dobrushin
contraction; data processing inequality; layer ablation; identifiability;
epistasis; sub-additivity; ablation arity.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement}

A $24$-layer transformer language model was subjected to the following protocol.
For each layer $j\in\{0,\dots,23\}$ independently, attention in layer $j$ was
restricted to an oracle top-$k$ set of keys ($k=16$ and $k=32$) while every
other layer was left fully intact; performance was recorded over held-out
windows at fixed context length. The resulting profile --- one number per layer
--- was almost perfectly flat.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
statistic & $k=16$\\
\midrule
best layer & $1.0013$ (layer $13$)\\
worst layer & $0.9953$ (layer $12$)\\
total spread & $0.6$ points\\
minimum over all layers & $\ge 0.995$\\
final layer $L_{23}$ & $1.0008$ --- the best in the stack\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

At $k=32$ the profile was flatter still. Two structural hypotheses stated in
advance --- that the tail layers are individually critical, and that the map
from layer index to importance is non-uniform --- were declared refuted, and the
verdict recorded was that no single layer is the bottleneck.

Two facts sit uneasily beside that verdict. First, four independent prior
experiments in the same programme had established the tail layers $L_{22},
L_{23}$ as categorically different from the rest of the stack along several
unrelated axes. Second, joint pruning of \emph{all} layers at $k=16$ cost
$1.7\%$, whereas the sum of the individual solo costs predicts roughly $4.8\%$:
interactions are strongly sub-additive.

\subsection{The question and the answer}

Both tensions concern what a solo-ablation profile is evidence \emph{for}. The
question admits an exact answer once one fixes a model in which ``layer'',
``ablation'' and ``damage'' are mathematical objects. We fix the smallest such
model --- finite state spaces, Markov channels, total variation --- and answer
it completely, in three parts. \emph{Negatively:} the solo profile does not
determine the joint cost, in either direction, even at the measured depth and
even when the profile is perfectly flat. \emph{Explanatorily:} flatness is the
generic consequence of downstream contraction, which attenuates a layer's damage
geometrically in its distance from the output. \emph{Constructively:} the exact
converse holds at a lossless suffix, the sub-additivity ratio estimates the
contraction coefficient, and the minimal arity at which a layer's damage becomes
visible above a given resolution is a closed-form function of that coefficient.

\section{The model}

Throughout, $\alpha,\beta$ denote finite non-empty sets and all probability
weights are rational, so every quantity below is exactly computable.

\begin{definition}[Distribution]
A \emph{distribution} on $\alpha$ is $\mu:\alpha\to\mathbb{Q}$ with $\mu(a)\ge0$
and $\sum_a\mu(a)=1$. Write $\Dist(\alpha)$ for the set of these; immediately
$\mu(a)\le1$ for every $a$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Channel]
A \emph{channel} $K:\alpha\rightsquigarrow\beta$ is a map $K:\alpha\to
\Dist(\beta)$, i.e.\ a row-stochastic matrix. Its \emph{pushforward} is
$(K_*\mu)(b)=\sum_a\mu(a)K(a)(b)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total variation]
$\dtv(\mu,\nu)=\tfrac12\sum_{a}|\mu(a)-\nu(a)|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\dtv$ is a metric on $\Dist(\alpha)$ with values in $[0,1]$, vanishing exactly
when $\mu=\nu$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Non-negativity, symmetry and the triangle inequality are termwise consequences
of the corresponding facts for $|\cdot|$. For the bound, $|\mu(a)-\nu(a)|\le
\mu(a)+\nu(a)$ pointwise by case analysis on the sign; summing gives $2$. A sum
of non-negative terms vanishes iff each term does.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
For $a\in\alpha$ let $\mathrm{dirac}(a)$ be the point mass at $a$. The
\emph{identity channel} is $\idch(a)=\mathrm{dirac}(a)$; the \emph{constant
channel} $\constch_c$ sends every input to $c$; for a bijection $e$ the
\emph{permutation channel} is $\permch_e(a)=\mathrm{dirac}(e(a))$.
\end{definition}

One checks $\idch_*\mu=\mu$ and $(\constch_c)_*\mu=c$. On $\{0,1\}$ we write
$\Bern(t)$ for the law $(1-t,t)$ and record
\begin{equation}\label{eq:tvbern}
\dtv\big(\Bern(s),\Bern(t)\big)=|s-t|.
\end{equation}

\begin{definition}[Stack and ablation]
A \emph{stack} is a list $F=(f_0,\dots,f_{n-1})$ of channels $\alpha
\rightsquigarrow\alpha$ acting by $\chainop(F,\mu)=(f_{n-1})_*\cdots(f_0)_*\mu$,
so that $\chainop(F_1\frown F_2,\mu)=\chainop(F_2,\chainop(F_1,\mu))$. An
\emph{ablation} at a set $S$ replaces $f_j$ by a prescribed $p_j$ for $j\in S$;
the \emph{arity} of the experiment is $|S|$. We write $F[j\mapsto p]$ and
$F[j\mapsto p,k\mapsto q]$ for arity $1$ and $2$.
\end{definition}

\section{Core inequalities}

\begin{theorem}[Data-processing inequality]\label{thm:dpi}
For every channel $K$ and all $\mu,\nu$, $\dtv(K_*\mu,K_*\nu)\le\dtv(\mu,\nu)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix $b$. Since $(K_*\mu)(b)-(K_*\nu)(b)=\sum_a(\mu(a)-\nu(a))K(a)(b)$, the
triangle inequality and $K(a)(b)\ge0$ give $|(K_*\mu)(b)-(K_*\nu)(b)|\le
\sum_a|\mu(a)-\nu(a)|K(a)(b)$. Sum over $b$, exchange the order of summation and
use $\sum_bK(a)(b)=1$.
\end{proof}

Applying Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} layer by layer gives $\dtv(\chainop(L,\mu),
\chainop(L,\nu))\le\dtv(\mu,\nu)$ for every stack $L$.

\begin{theorem}[Perturbation inequality]\label{thm:perturb}
$\dtv(f_*\mu,p_*\mu)\le\sum_a\mu(a)\,\dtv(f(a),p(a))$. In particular, if
$\dtv(f(a),p(a))\le\varepsilon$ for \emph{every} $a$, then
$\dtv(f_*\mu,p_*\mu)\le\varepsilon$ for every $\mu$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Identical bookkeeping to Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi}, factoring $\mu(a)$ out of the
absolute value; the uniform form follows from $\sum_a\mu(a)\varepsilon=
\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

The uniform hypothesis --- closeness at \emph{every} input, not merely on
average against one $\mu$ --- is the hinge of the paper: it is the difference
between a per-layer budget that composes and one that does not.

\begin{theorem}[Context-shift inequality]\label{thm:shift}
$\dtv(f_*\mu,p_*\mu)\le\dtv(f_*\nu,p_*\nu)+2\,\dtv(\mu,\nu)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two applications of the triangle inequality route $f_*\mu\to f_*\nu\to p_*\nu\to
p_*\mu$; Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} bounds each outer leg by $\dtv(\mu,\nu)$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:shift} is the exact quantitative form of the epistasis puzzle:
a solo ablation evaluates a layer's damage at the \emph{intact} upstream state,
a joint ablation at a \emph{drifted} one, and the discrepancy is controlled
entirely by the drift, at a price of $2$ per unit.

\section{Three cost functionals and the epistasis budget}

Fix a stack $F$ of length $n$, an input law $\mu$, and a pruning
$P=(p_0,\dots,p_{n-1})$.

\begin{definition}\label{def:costs}
Let $\nu_j=\chainop(F_{<j},\mu)$ be the \emph{intact upstream state} at layer
$j$. Then
\begin{itemize}
\item the \emph{point cost} is $\pt_j=\dtv\big((f_j)_*\nu_j,(p_j)_*\nu_j\big)$;
\item the \emph{solo cost} is $\so_j=\dtv\big(\chainop(F,\mu),
\chainop(F[j\mapsto p_j],\mu)\big)$;
\item the \emph{joint cost} is $\jt=\dtv\big(\chainop(F,\mu),
\chainop(P,\mu)\big)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

The experiment reports $(\so_j)_j$; deployment cares about $\jt$; mechanistic
interpretation wants $(\pt_j)_j$.

\begin{theorem}[Masking, weak form]\label{thm:soloLEpoint}
$\so_j\le\pt_j$ for every $j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both output laws arise by pushing $(f_j)_*\nu_j$ and $(p_j)_*\nu_j$ through the
\emph{same} suffix; apply data processing along it.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Hybrid cost]
$\hyb((),P,\mu)=0$ and $\hyb(f{::}F',p{::}P',\mu)=\dtv(f_*\mu,p_*\mu)+
\hyb(F',P',p_*\mu)$. Each summand is a \emph{contextual} cost, evaluated at the
already-pruned upstream state.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Hybrid bound]\label{thm:hybrid}
If $|F|=|P|$ then $\jt\le\hyb(F,P,\mu)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on length. For $F=f{::}F'$, $P=p{::}P'$, the triangle inequality
routes $\chainop(F',f_*\mu)\to\chainop(F',p_*\mu)\to\chainop(P',p_*\mu)$; the
first leg is at most $\dtv(f_*\mu,p_*\mu)$ by data processing along $F'$, the
second is the inductive hypothesis at $p_*\mu$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Depth-additive budget]\label{cor:depth}
If $\dtv(f_i(a),p_i(a))\le\varepsilon$ for every layer $i$ and \emph{every}
state $a$, then $\jt\le n\varepsilon$.
\end{corollary}

This is the only hypothesis under which a per-layer budget genuinely adds up,
and it is uniform over upstream states; solo ablation measures the perturbation
at exactly one state. Combining Theorems~\ref{thm:shift} and~\ref{thm:hybrid}
gives, at depth two, the drift-explicit budget
\begin{equation}\label{eq:depth2}
\jt\;\le\;3\pt_0+\pt_1,
\end{equation}
exhibiting the asymmetry: damage created early is charged three times, because
it also displaces the context in which later layers are evaluated.

\section{Non-identifiability}

\begin{definition}[Witness family]
For $n\ge0$ let $F^{(n)}=(\underbrace{\idch,\dots,\idch}_{n},\constch_{\delta_0})$,
$n$ transparent layers followed by one totally forgetful layer emitting the
point mass $\delta_0$ at state $0$. For $t\in[0,1]$ the pruning $P^{(n)}(t)$
replaces each transparent layer by $\constch_{\Bern(t)}$ and the forgetful layer
by $\idch$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Perfectly flat solo profile]\label{thm:flat}
For every depth $n+1$, every $t\in[0,1]$ and every layer $j\le n$, the solo cost
of $F^{(n)}$ under $P^{(n)}(t)$ at layer $j$ is exactly $0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $j<n$ the ablated layer precedes the forgetful final layer, which resets the
state to $\delta_0$ regardless. If $j=n$ the ablation replaces the forgetful
layer by the identity while all upstream layers are intact identities, so the
output is the input $\delta_0$. Either way the output is unchanged.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:joint}
The joint cost of $F^{(n)}$ versus $P^{(n)}(t)$ is exactly $t$; and for every
non-final layer $j<n$ the point cost is exactly $t$. Hence
Theorem~\ref{thm:soloLEpoint} is saturated in the worst possible way: the true
per-layer damage profile is flat at $t$ while the measured profile is flat
at $0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The pruned stack outputs $\Bern(t)$ and the intact stack $\delta_0=\Bern(0)$;
apply \eqref{eq:tvbern}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-identifiability at the measured depth]\label{thm:nonid}
There exist stacks $F,P,Q$ of length exactly $24$ such that the solo cost of $F$
under $P$ is $0$ at every one of the $24$ layers; the solo cost of $F$ under $Q$
is $0$ at every one of the $24$ layers; the joint cost of $F$ versus $P$ is
exactly $0.017$; and the joint cost of $F$ versus $Q$ is exactly $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $F=F^{(23)}$, $P=P^{(23)}(0.017)$, $Q=P^{(23)}(1)$ and apply
Theorems~\ref{thm:flat} and~\ref{thm:joint}.
\end{proof}

Two prunings of one network with literally identical solo profiles --- flatter
than any real measurement --- whose joint costs are the benign laboratory figure
and total destruction of the output law.

\begin{theorem}[Cancellation]\label{thm:cancel}
Let $F=(\idch,\idch)$ on $\{0,1\}$ and $P=(\mathrm{flip},\mathrm{flip})$ with
$\mathrm{flip}$ the deterministic state swap. Then both solo costs equal $1$ and
the joint cost equals $0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[No two-sided law]
There is no valid inequality $\jt\le\sum_j\so_j$ and none of the form
$\jt\ge\max_j\so_j$. The solo profile bounds the joint cost neither above nor
below; in particular the observed sub-additivity is a contingent property of the
network measured, not a law of pruning.
\end{corollary}

\section{Why flat profiles are generic: Dobrushin masking}

\begin{definition}[Dobrushin coefficient]
$K$ has coefficient at most $\delta$ if $\dtv(K(a),K(b))\le\delta$ for all
$a,b$; the least such value is $\delta(K)=\max_{a,b}\dtv(K(a),K(b))\in[0,1]$.
\end{definition}

$\delta(K)=0$ characterises constant channels, and $\delta(\permch_e)=1$ on at
least two states.

\begin{theorem}[Dobrushin contraction]\label{thm:dob}
If $\delta(K)\le\delta$ then $\dtv(K_*\mu,K_*\nu)\le\delta\,\dtv(\mu,\nu)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $\mu-\nu=\sigma^+-\sigma^-$ for the positive and negative parts of the
signed difference. Both have the same total mass $r=\dtv(\mu,\nu)$ since
$\sum_a(\mu(a)-\nu(a))=0$. If $r=0$ then $\mu=\nu$. Otherwise normalise to
probability measures $\hat\sigma^\pm=\sigma^\pm/r$, so $K_*\mu-K_*\nu=
r(K_*\hat\sigma^+-K_*\hat\sigma^-)$. Since $K_*\hat\sigma^\pm$ are mixtures of
rows of $K$ and total variation is jointly convex, their distance is at most
$\max_{a,b}\dtv(K(a),K(b))\le\delta$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:suffix}
A stack of $m$ layers each of coefficient at most $\delta$ contracts total
variation by $\delta^m$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Masking theorem]\label{thm:mask}
If $m_j$ layers follow $j$, each of coefficient at most $\delta$, then
$\so_j\le\delta^{\,m_j}\pt_j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Contraction masks maximal damage]\label{cor:maskmax}
With $\delta=1/2$ and at least $11$ layers after $j$, layer $j$ may destroy its
own output law completely --- point cost $1$, the maximum possible --- while its
solo cost is at most $2^{-11}<0.0005$, an order of magnitude below the $0.006$
total spread reported by the measurement.
\end{corollary}

A stack of depth $24$ with an ordinary amount of per-layer forgetting therefore
returns a flat solo profile whether or not it contains a bottleneck: the
measurement is a statement about the contraction of the stack, not about the
importance of its layers. Section~\ref{sec:frac} shows Theorem~\ref{thm:mask} is
attained exactly, so this is not an artefact of a loose estimate.

\section{When the measurement is faithful}

\begin{definition}
A layer is \emph{lossless} if it is a permutation channel.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\dtv((\permch_e)_*\mu,(\permch_e)_*\nu)=\dtv(\mu,\nu)$; a stack of lossless
layers is a total variation isometry.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Identifiability at a lossless suffix]\label{thm:lossless}
If every layer after $j$ is lossless then $\so_j=\pt_j$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The final layer is always faithful]\label{cor:last}
For $j$ the last index, $\so_j=\pt_j$ unconditionally.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Interpolation]
If the suffix after $j$ contracts total variation by a factor $c$, then
$\so_j\le c\,\pt_j$. The endpoint $c=1$ is attained by a lossless suffix and the
endpoint $c=0$ by the witness family of Theorem~\ref{thm:flat}.
\end{theorem}

The entire range from ``the solo profile measures everything'' to ``the solo
profile measures nothing'' is therefore parameterised by one number --- the
contraction coefficient of the downstream suffix --- with no reference to which
layer is important. Layer importance and solo measurability are independent
quantities.

The measurement reported its smallest damage at the final layer $L_{23}$, which
by Corollary~\ref{cor:last} is exactly the one layer where solo cost equals
point cost with no loss. That number is trustworthy: the tail's own perturbation
genuinely is small. It is fully compatible with the tail being indispensable,
since indispensability of the kind established by neighbouring experiments lives
in the interaction between the tail and upstream representations --- the drift
term of Theorem~\ref{thm:shift}.

\section{Sub-additivity and its sharp constant}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric budget]\label{thm:geom}
Let every intact layer of a depth-$n$ stack have Dobrushin coefficient at most
$\delta\in[0,1]$, and let $\dtv(f_i(a),p_i(a))\le c$ for every layer $i$ and
every state $a$. Then
\[
\jt\;\le\;c\sum_{i<n}\delta^{\,i}\;\le\;\frac{c}{1-\delta}\quad(\delta<1),
\]
a bound independent of the depth.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Refine Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid}: the damage created at layer $i$ is at most $c$
by uniform perturbation, but must traverse the intact layers $i+1,\dots,n-1$,
which damp it by $\delta^{\,n-1-i}$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:suffix}. Sum and
re-index.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
At $\delta=1/2$ and any depth, $\jt\le2c$ against the additive prediction $nc$;
at depth $24$ a genuine factor-$12$ gain whenever $c>0$.
\end{corollary}

The hypothesis cannot be dropped: the witness family has all solo costs $0$ and
joint cost $1$. An upper bound alone, however, cannot convert the measured ratio
into a statement about the network. The bound is attained.

\begin{definition}[Affine two-state layer]
For $\delta,s\ge0$ with $s+\delta\le1$, let $A_{\delta,s}$ be the channel on
$\{0,1\}$ with rows $A_{\delta,s}(x)=\Bern(s+\delta x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:aff}
$(A_{\delta,s})_*\Bern(q)=\Bern(s+\delta q)$, $\delta(A_{\delta,s})=\delta$, and
$\dtv(A_{\delta,s}(a),A_{\delta,s'}(a))=|s-s'|$ for every $a$. The $n$-fold
iterate acts on the Bernoulli parameter by $q\mapsto\delta^nq+s\sum_{i<n}
\delta^{\,i}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
For every $\delta,c\ge0$ with $c+\delta\le1$ and every depth $n$, the stack of
$n$ copies of $A_{\delta,0}$, pruned to $n$ copies of $A_{\delta,c}$, has intact
contraction exactly $\delta$, uniform per-layer budget exactly $c$, and joint
damage exactly $c\sum_{i<n}\delta^{\,i}$. Hence no constant smaller than $1$ can
be inserted in Theorem~\ref{thm:geom}.
\end{theorem}

Because the bound is attained, the sub-additivity ratio $R$ between the additive
prediction and the joint cost becomes an estimator:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:estimator}
R=\frac{nc}{c\sum_{i<n}\delta^{\,i}}=\frac{n(1-\delta)}{1-\delta^{\,n}}.
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[The measured pair, reproduced]\label{thm:estimator}
At depth $24$, intact-layer contraction $\delta=8/9$ and uniform per-layer
budget $c=1/500$ give an additive prediction $24\cdot(1/500)=4.8\%$, exactly the
reported figure, and joint pruning damage strictly between $1.69\%$ and
$1.70\%$, exactly the reported $1.7\%$ --- with a stack whose $24$ layers are
all identical, so that no per-layer hierarchy is involved.
\end{theorem}

