\section{The arity hierarchy}

\subsection{Arity two suffices when one layer does the masking}

\begin{definition}[Differential pair cost]
For $j<k$, the \emph{differential pair cost} at $j$ relative to $k$ is
$\dtv\big(\chainop(F[k\mapsto q],\mu),\chainop(F[j\mapsto p,k\mapsto q],\mu)\big)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Pair identifiability]\label{thm:pair}
If ablating layer $k$ makes the suffix after $j$ lossless, then the differential
pair cost at $j$ relative to $k$ equals $\pt_j$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Ablating a layer strictly after $j$ leaves the upstream state $\nu_j$ unchanged,
so both runs agree up to layer $j$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:lossless} to the
modified suffix.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:pair24}
At depth $24$ there is an intact stack $F$ and two prunings $p,q$ of the
transparent layers such that every solo cost of either pruning is $0$, while for
every transparent layer $j<23$ the differential pair costs with the tail layer
$23$ are $0.017$ and $1$ respectively --- a separation of $0.983$ where arity
$1$ achieves $0$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Arity two is not enough in general}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:twomask}
Let $T^{(n)}$ consist of $n$ transparent layers followed by \emph{two} totally
forgetful layers, with ablation replacing a transparent layer by
$\constch_{\Bern(t)}$ and a forgetful layer by $\idch$. Then for every $n$ and
every $t\in[0,1]$: (i) every ablation of a single layer costs exactly $0$;
(ii) every ablation of a pair of layers costs exactly $0$; (iii) ablating a
transparent layer together with both forgetful layers costs exactly $t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For (i) and (ii): an ablation of size at most $2$ either leaves one forgetful
layer intact --- and a surviving forgetful layer resets the state, erasing
everything upstream --- or ablates both, in which case no transparent layer was
touched and the stack is a chain of identities on $\delta_0$. For (iii), with
both maskers turned into identities the modified transparent layer's output
$\Bern(t)$ reaches the output; apply \eqref{eq:tvbern}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The general hierarchy}

\begin{theorem}[No fixed arity is sound]\label{thm:hierarchy}
Fix $m\ge1$ and let the stack consist of $n$ transparent layers followed by $m$
forgetful layers. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Blindness.} For every ablation set $S$ with $|S|\le m$ the output
law is exactly unchanged: the measured cost is $0$.
\item \textbf{Recovery.} Ablating all $m$ maskers together with one transparent
layer (arity $m+1$) returns exactly the pruning strength $t$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, for every $m$ with $n+m=24$ there are two prunings of a
depth-$24$ stack that agree at $0$ on all experiments of arity $\le m$ and
differ by $0.983$ at arity $m+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Part (1) is a pigeonhole. Either some masker survives, in which case that
constant layer resets the state to $\delta_0$ and every layer after it is either
an identity or another constant $\delta_0$ layer, so the output is $\delta_0$
regardless of anything upstream; or all $m$ maskers are ablated, and since
$|S|\le m$ the set $S$ is exactly the set of maskers, so no transparent layer
was modified and the stack is a chain of identities on $\delta_0$. Part (2) is
immediate.
\end{proof}

Since $m$ is arbitrary, raising the arity of an ablation protocol by any fixed
amount does not make it sound: the minimal informative arity is not a universal
constant but grows with the number of layers over which downstream forgetting is
spread.

\section{Fractional masking and the resolution threshold}\label{sec:frac}

\begin{definition}[Probe stack]
Fix $\delta\in[0,1]$, $c\ge0$ with $c+\delta\le1$, and $k\le m$. The probe stack
consists of the layer under study --- $A_{\delta,s}$ with $s=0$ intact and $s=c$
pruned, so its point cost is exactly $c$ --- followed by $m-k$ intact masking
layers (copies of $A_{\delta,0}$) and $k$ ablated ones (replaced by the
identity). The experiment has arity $k+1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact probe cost]\label{thm:probe}
The measured cost of the arity-$(k+1)$ experiment is exactly
$\delta^{\,m-k}\cdot c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each surviving masking layer multiplies the Bernoulli parameter gap by exactly
$\delta$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:aff} with $s=0$); each ablated layer transmits it
unchanged.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
At $k=0$ the solo cost is exactly $\delta^m$ times the point cost, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:mask} is attained; the measured cost is monotone in the arity,
each extra ablated masking layer multiplying it by $1/\delta$; and at $k=m$ the
measurement equals the point cost exactly.
\end{corollary}

Hence the smallest arity at which a layer's damage is visible at resolution
$\varepsilon$ is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:threshold}
1+m-\left\lceil\log_\delta\!\big(\varepsilon/\pt\big)\right\rceil.
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Resolution threshold]\label{thm:threshold}
Take $\delta=1/2$, $m=11$ masking layers, point cost $1/2$, and resolution
$\varepsilon=0.006$ (the reported spread). Then every experiment of arity at
most $5$ reports a cost strictly below $\varepsilon$, and the arity-$6$
experiment reports a cost strictly above it.
\end{theorem}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccl}
\toprule
$k$ (maskers ablated) & arity $k+1$ & measured cost & visible?\\
\midrule
$0$ (solo) & $1$ & $1/4096\approx0.000244$ & no\\
$1$ & $2$ & $0.000488$ & no\\
$2$ & $3$ & $0.000977$ & no\\
$3$ & $4$ & $0.001953$ & no\\
$4$ & $5$ & $0.003906$ & no\\
$5$ & $6$ & $0.007813$ & \textbf{yes}\\
$11$ (all) & $12$ & $0.5=\pt$ & yes\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The solo measurement here is three orders of magnitude below the damage it is
meant to detect. The transition is sharp and depends on the contraction
coefficient alone: halving $\delta$ moves the threshold by one layer, and
$\delta=0$ pushes it past every finite arity, recovering
Theorem~\ref{thm:hierarchy}.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Exact damage propagation.}
Given a stack of $n$ channels on $|\alpha|$ states, an input law and a pruning,
compute the point, solo and joint costs exactly. Running the stack costs
$O(n|\alpha|^2)$ operations; the full solo profile costs $O(n^2|\alpha|^2)$
naively, or $O(n|\alpha|^2)$ with prefix/suffix caching. Exactness is available
because all weights are rational.

\paragraph{B. Contraction estimation from the sub-additivity ratio.}
Given the additive prediction $A=\sum_j\so_j$ and the measured joint cost $J$,
solve \eqref{eq:estimator}, $A/J=n(1-\delta)/(1-\delta^n)$, for $\delta\in[0,1)$
by bisection. The right-hand side is continuous and strictly decreasing from $n$
at $\delta=0$ to $1$ as $\delta\to1$, so the root is unique whenever
$1<A/J<n$; bisection converges in $O(\log(1/\text{tol}))$ evaluations.

\paragraph{C. Minimal informative arity.}
Given $\delta$, a masking depth $m$, a hypothesised point cost and a resolution
$\varepsilon$, return the smallest $k+1$ with $\delta^{m-k}\pt>\varepsilon$ ---
formula \eqref{eq:threshold}, or a linear scan in $O(m)$ steps. If no $k\le m$
qualifies, no experiment confined to that suffix can detect the layer at that
resolution.

\section{Discussion}

The reported verdict is true as a statement about the measurement, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:nonid} shows that as a statement about the network it is
unsupported: two networks differing maximally in joint pruning behaviour produce
identical, perfectly flat solo profiles. Theorem~\ref{thm:mask} and
Corollary~\ref{cor:maskmax} show flatness is the default expectation for any
contracting stack of depth $24$. The hypothesis that layer importance is
non-uniform was not refuted; it was tested with an instrument that provably
returns a flat reading either way.

The one faithful component is the tail: by Corollary~\ref{cor:last} the final
layer's solo cost equals its point cost, so its best-in-stack score is a genuine
statement about that layer's own perturbation --- and compatible with the tail
being functionally indispensable through interaction.

The gap between $4.8\%$ additive and $1.7\%$ joint is genuine information about
a global property: by Theorems~\ref{thm:sharp} and~\ref{thm:estimator} it is
reproduced exactly by $24$ identical layers with contraction $8/9$ and budget
$1/500$.

Five design implications follow. (1) Report contraction alongside the profile;
the ratio $A/J$ estimates $\delta$, which determines how much of the point
profile the solo profile could have seen. (2) Compute the resolution threshold
\eqref{eq:threshold} \emph{before} running the experiment; running below it
guarantees a flat chart independently of the truth. (3) Fixed-arity protocols
cannot be made sound by increasing the arity (Theorem~\ref{thm:hierarchy});
sound protocols must be adaptive, co-ablating estimated maskers as in
Theorem~\ref{thm:pair}. (4) Only uniform-in-context budgets compose across depth
(Corollary~\ref{cor:depth}); a budget certified at one upstream state is not
enough. (5) The negative conclusion about mixed-precision serving survives, but
because the joint cost was separately measured at $1.7\%$, not because the
profile was flat.

\paragraph{Scope.} The model is finite-state, Markov, and measures damage in
total variation; real layers are deterministic maps on continuous
representations, and total variation is coarser than task loss. What transfers
is the structure of the argument --- data processing, contraction, and the
identity of the three cost functionals hold for any composition of
information-losing maps under any monotone divergence. What does not transfer
automatically is the numerical calibration, which fits a one-parameter model to
a two-number summary and should be read as an order-of-magnitude estimator. The
witnesses are extremal by design: that is what makes them proofs of
impossibility. The claim is not that a trained network resembles them, but that
a measurement which cannot distinguish them is not evidence about which of them
one is holding.

\section{Future directions}

\emph{Adaptive protocols with proven guarantees.} Theorem~\ref{thm:pair} shows
co-ablating the masker recovers the point cost exactly; what is the optimal
adaptive strategy under a fixed budget of forward passes?

\emph{Beyond total variation.} The masking theorem holds for any $f$-divergence
with a contraction coefficient; which sharp constants survive for KL divergence
or for task loss?

\emph{Interaction-aware importance measures.} The drift term of
Theorem~\ref{thm:shift} is precisely what solo measurement discards; a
functional estimating drift directly would target the quantity in which the
tail's specialness appears to live.

\emph{Pairwise and joint tail ablations at scale.} Theorem~\ref{thm:pair24}
predicts a pair ablation including the tail separates hypotheses that solo
ablation cannot --- directly testable.

\emph{Deeper stacks.} Theorem~\ref{thm:geom} gives a depth-independent budget
$c/(1-\delta)$; if \eqref{eq:estimator} returns similar $\delta$ at larger
depths, joint pruning damage should saturate rather than grow with depth.

\emph{Non-uniform contraction spectra.} Layerwise coefficients $\delta_i$
replace $\delta^m$ by $\prod_{i>j}\delta_i$ throughout, making the threshold
\eqref{eq:threshold} layer-specific and the masking profile non-uniform even
when the solo profile is flat --- arguably the most realistic model of a trained
stack.

\section{Conclusion}

Layer ablation is a low-pass filter on depth. It reports faithfully at the
output end of a stack --- exactly and unconditionally at the final layer --- and
attenuates geometrically upstream, at a rate set by the network's contraction
coefficient and nothing else. A flat solo profile is consistent with any joint
behaviour whatsoever; conversely solo costs can both be maximal while the joint
cost vanishes. The observed sub-additivity is real information, but about how
much the network forgets. And raising the ablation arity fails in general: for
every $m$ there is a depth-$24$ stack on which every experiment of arity $\le m$
returns exactly $0$ while a single experiment of arity $m+1$ returns the true
damage in full. The instrument is not broken --- it is measuring the contraction
of the stack, and its resolution as an importance probe is computable in advance
from that same quantity.

